


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: rodin
@title: What Digital Projects Are Good For
@presenter: rodin
@abstract: 

After the inaugural ideas and the grant applications, after
the coding and the web design, it bears asking: What can a
digital-humanities project do? What is it good for beyond
demonstrating that it might, someday, do something? By
“do something” I mean not merely “accomplish
something in the digital domain,” but rather “help
solve a non-trivial—and non-digital—problem.”

This paper offers some answers using the <i>Josquin Research
Project</i> (josquin.stanford.edu) and <i>Mapping the Musical
Renaissance</i> (renaissancemapping.org). Platforms like these
can be used to solve problems of attribution, assist with
music editing, and address issues of geography and
biography—that is, to tackle real research questions.

@@END: PRESENTATION



